## 6. Geoapify Integration

Endpoint: `https://api.geoapify.com/v2/places`

Request includes: category, lat, lng, radius, limit, API key.

**API key security:**
```
backend/.env → process.env.GEOAPIFY_API_KEY → mapController → Geoapify
```
- Key stays on the backend only — never in `Emergency.jsx` / `TripMap.jsx`.
- `.env` must be in `.gitignore`.

**Search radius:** 25 km (Geoapify needs meters → `const radius = 25000;`)

**Result limit:** Up to 50 places from backend; frontend shows only the nearest few to keep UI clean.

---

## 7. Backend Response Shape

Geoapify's feature objects are simplified before sending to frontend:

```javascript
const places = data.features.map((item) => ({
  id: item.properties.place_id,
  lat: item.properties.lat,
  lon: item.properties.lon,
  name: item.properties.name || item.properties.address_line1
}));
```

This decouples the frontend from Geoapify's raw response format.

---

## 8. Distance Calculation — Haversine

For each resource, distance between user `(lat1, lng1)` and resource `(lat2, lng2)` is computed using the **Haversine formula** (great-circle distance).

**Why not Euclidean distance?** Euclidean assumes a flat plane; lat/lng are coordinates on a curved Earth surface, so Haversine is more accurate.

> **Interview answer:** I used Haversine because I needed the geographic distance between the user and nearby resources. Since input is lat/lng, Haversine is more appropriate than Euclidean distance.

Then sorted nearest-first:
```javascript
places.sort((a, b) => a.distance - b.distance);
```

